Now I understand the loop mechanism precisely:

`independent_supervisor_100.sh` runs an infinite `while :; do` loop that calls `launch worker "$n"` for n in 01..100. `launch()` returns early if the tmux session `worker-$n` exists (`tmux has-session -t "$key" && return`). So when a worker session exits, the next iteration (every 20 s) relaunches a fresh agent for that slot with the same prompt. That's why worker-041 keeps getting relaunched: its tmux session ended, so the supervisor recreates it every 20 seconds with the same prompt.

So the loop is by design of this supervisor: any worker that exits gets relaunched. The only way to stop it is to kill the supervisor loop or remove/retire the slot. Previous messages asked "supervisor must stop relaunching the completed slot" but nothing changed — because the supervisor is a dumb loop, and the worker can't communicate with it except by not exiting... wait, actually: the supervisor checks `tmux has-session -t "$key"`; if the worker's tmux session persists, it won't relaunch. But the prompt says "then exit", and dsh exits when done, ending the session → relaunch. So each worker that completes gets an infinite stream of relaunches. That's the actual mechanism.

